00:27:06.950 --> 00:27:28.420
<v Speaker 1>it is not just a, I'm not just a megaphone making your voice louder. I do some of that. But also I'm trying to figure out if enough people say something, I'm like, OK, what's going on? What is the concern? Because one of the things I've learned over time is people tell you why they are unhappy, but they don't necessarily always tell you what it is that's causing that happiness.</v>

00:27:30.220 --> 00:27:44.460
<v Speaker 1>Like a lot of times, it's funny, one of the trainings there was when I was a counselor many years ago is that kids will come to you. And I tend to like working with the younger kids is that kids will come to you crying.</v>

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:50.480
<v Speaker 1>And one of the things you have to understand is you got to figure out, um,</v>

00:27:51.560 --> 00:28:06.670
<v Speaker 1>you know, what is why? Why are they crying? What is causing to be upset? And some kids will be very forthcoming. Some kids will tell you exactly what's wrong, but other kids just sort of communicate that they're upset and don't tell you why they're upset</v>

00:28:07.350 --> 00:28:25.760
<v Speaker 1>and that that is very common. The way feedback happens that sometimes people are really exact, you did this thing, I don't like this thing, change this thing very clear. Other times people are just like, I don't like this set. I'm like, OK, well, what about the set? Don't you let you know? I want to get a little bit of dialogue. And the one nice thing about</v>

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:30.720
<v Speaker 1>sort of how media works now is that there is an, there is a two way,</v>

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:52.650
<v Speaker 1>they call two way addressability, but that not only do I talk to you, but I can listen to you, you can talk to me and there's back and forth. I appreciate that a lot. 1 things I love in my blog is somebody also questioned, I'll answer it and somebody else will like then ask a second question based on my answer. So that there's sort of a dialogue that got formed between the blog and myself and I really appreciate that.</v>

00:28:54.730 --> 00:29:08.520
<v Speaker 1>The final thing as far as being a spokesperson is what I would call appearances is like for example, Haskins good example this year is we were doing something. It was the first time we were there. We had a lot of Magic players.</v>

00:29:10.070 --> 00:29:35.910
<v Speaker 1>I work mostly on the West Coast, so I'm not on the East Coast very often. I haven't been there in 10 years. And that part is also being spokesperson, is just interacting with the public. I do that online, but part of it also is just doing it in person. I don't get out as much as the one part of my job that I don't quite as much as they used to just because of my family. But I do try to make it out to a couple of events every year. And when I'm at events, I really try super hard to meet as many people as possible.</v>

00:29:37.510 --> 00:30:07.940
<v Speaker 1>Part of that is talking to people, shaking hands, taking pictures, signing autographs, you know, just interacting with the fans in a direct way, which I enjoy a lot. It's a lot of fun. I mean, one of the things to keep in mind here, sort of my message of today is this is kind of something I didn't set out to do per se. I never sort of set it up. You know what, I'm going to be the spokesperson or spokesperson for Magic. Kind of what happened was I just did the things I like to do, which is communicate and share information and then interact.</v>

00:30:08.070 --> 00:30:37.900
<v Speaker 1>And I kind of grew into the job because kind of I was doing what the job required somebody to do and I was doing it on my own free will already. But anyway, it's a fun job. I actually like all the pieces of it. I like talking to all of you. I like doing interviews. I like meeting people. I even like being the lightning rod, even though there are days that it's harder than others. I will say, by the way, I often say this, but if you have something to say, if you're trying to give feedback, my one piece of</v>

00:30:38.070 --> 00:30:52.550
<v Speaker 1>could be advice. One is be constructive in the feedback. Not that you can't say something's wrong, but be polite, be kind. You don't have to yell at me when something's wrong. You can calmly talk to me and tell me something's wrong. Number 2 is</v>

00:30:54.860 --> 00:31:23.490
<v Speaker 1>if you can identify what the problem is, that's the most valuable thing to me. What is wrong and why don't you like it? Those are the two things in motion. Important. What is wrong and why don't you like it? I don't mind you giving me suggestions of how you would fix it. Be aware that because you don't know all the constraints, most of the time your fix doesn't necessarily work. But I'm very interested in hearing what the problems are because I have a team of very qualified people to help solve those problems. And that the best thing you guys can do is help me understand what those problems are</v>

00:31:23.620 --> 00:31:53.770
<v Speaker 1>so that I can communicate that to the people that need to and we can fix the problems. But anyway, so I've actually been here for a few minutes, give you guys your full podcast material. But anyway, that is what it's to be the spokesperson. That's sort of how it became the spokesperson and what I do in my spokesperson job. But anyway, I'm now in a parking space. So we all know that means this is the end of my drive to work. Instead of talking magic, it's time for me to be taken to be making magic. I'll see you guys next time. Bye bye.</v>

